- op: discuss  # 2d79
  at: '2026-09-11T11:17:59Z'  # 2d79
  by: agent  # 2d79
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 2d79
  clock: 10  # 2d79
  nonce: 2d79  # 2d79
  message: "DONE — arc T3, PR #915 (dev 3f3d684166). The static-AST linter this item asked for, plus the Edge.id validator extension, plus the 26 live emitters the check found.\n\nWHAT SHIPPED: `hypergumbo_core/check_id_construction.py`, `scripts/check-id-construction`, `test_live_tree_passes` as the CI gate, and a `.githooks/pre-commit` step that runs whenever any package source is staged. ADR-0034 §'Enforcement' is updated in place — its 'Static check — known gap' section now describes what landed.\n\n**THE FILED SHAPE IS THE RAREST SPELLING OF THE DEFECT.** `Symbol(id=f\"...\")` at the constructor has EXACTLY ONE live instance. Measured over packages/*/src: of 470 `id=` arguments, 231 are a bare local, 168 are `make_symbol_id(...)`, 56 a module-local `_make_*_id`, 11 `make_file_id(...)`, and 1 an f-string. **20 of the f-strings hide behind a local**, so a check that stops at the constructor reaches 1 site in 470. The linter resolves three hops — the argument, one in-function local, one same-module helper — and stops there; a fourth needs real dataflow.\n\n**WHAT IS FORBIDDEN IS NOT THE F-STRING, IT IS THE UNGRAMMATICAL ID.** A synthetic linker stand-in has no source span and legitimately writes `0-0` by hand; 14 of the tree's 40 hand-built templates are conformant and stay. The rule is ADR-0036's grammar applied to the TEMPLATE, with an interpolated slot treated as OPAQUE rather than guessed at.\n\n**26 SITES REPAIRED**, all ids this project's OWN runtime validator classifies as malformed. Commonest: a bare line number where the grammar wants `start-end` (`grpc:{path}:{line}:{name}:{kind}`), which right-anchored parsing then reads as a span of '12'. Also `:{line}:0:` where the hyphen belongs (crypto_flow, message_dispatch, yjs_crdt), a name in the kind slot (ipc bridge_caller) and a URL path in it (openapi), slots ordered kind-before-span-before-name (bibtex), no span slot at all (bitbake), an illegal hyphen in the language slot (swift-objc), and `css:sha256:<digest>` — three segments the five-slot grammar cannot parse at all.\n\n**THREE OF THE 26 ARE SIBLINGS OF A SITE WI-zugob ALREADY REPAIRED IN THE SAME FILE.** That is the pattern the check exists to stop.\n\nTHE CSS REPAIR IS MEASURED END TO END, not asserted: on a two-file fixture the analyzer goes from four `css:sha256:<digest>` ids to four canonical ones, and the css `@import` edge REACHES THE SURVEY FOR THE FIRST TIME (0 -> 1 edge, both endpoints present as nodes) — the exact state `test_import_dst_ids_are_canonical` documents when it excludes css because 'their src is a hash-shaped symbol id … that the pipeline drops, so the edges are orphaned'.\n\n**THE DEFERRED Edge.id ITEM ASKED FOR THE WRONG GRAMMAR.** Phase 6 PR1 deferred 'extend _check_id_format to scan Edge.id for the same canonical shape it enforces on Symbol.id'. Checked against a live survey BEFORE implementing: every one of apollo-server's 18,283 edge ids is `edge:sha256:<16hex>`, a content digest, so that rule would have reported a 100% violation rate on every repository hypergumbo has ever analysed. The two are counterparts in ROLE — per-instance identity, against the structural edge_key/stable_id pair — and NOT in shape. Edge.id gets its own validator against what the factory actually mints, at both producer and runtime, and the wired-checks manifest guard caught the omission when I first forgot to disclose it.\n\nRESIDUAL FILED: ~15 per-language `_make_edge_id` helpers each re-spell the `edge:sha256:<16hex>` grammar by hand (WI-vuvah, P3). All currently agree — checked, not assumed — so it is the SHAPE of a defect rather than one."  # 2d79
